course: dictionary
lesson: 7056
title: Pastry: one word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can name something you can count and also a material you cannot count. «A table, with a large dish of tarts upon it» uses tarts, which are small sweet pastries with jam or fruit on top. You can count tarts: one tart, two tarts.
explain: Pastry is also the dough itself, made from flour, fat and water. You cannot count dough in that way, so you say some pastry or puff pastry. Pastry is rolled out flat to make pies and tarts.
explain: The words around pastry give you clues. In «pastry shop», the shop sells pastries you can count. In «pastry chef», a chef makes food from pastry. Try the swap test: if "a pastry" fits the sentence, pastry means the food you can count.
fact: A pastry, pastries means a small baked sweet food made with dough, like a croissant, a Danish or a tart.
fact: Pastry means the dough made of flour, fat and water, used for pies and tarts.
fact: «Puff pastry» is a light, flaky dough, and «la galette des rois» is a puff-pastry cake.
fact: Pastry is usually uncountable, so you say some pastry, not two pastry.
fact: «Un pain au chocolat» and «une chocolatine» are the same pastry, and people in different parts of France use different names.
exercise: «In the middle of the court is a table, with a large dish of tarts upon it.» What are tarts here? | Sharp knives for bread | Large dishes for soup | Tables with four legs | *Small sweet pastries with jam or fruit
exercise: Ella says pastry and a pastry mean the same. What is the difference? | *Pastry is the dough; a pastry is one small baked food | Pastry is a shop; a pastry is the baker | They mean exactly the same thing | A pastry is a loaf of bread
exercise: «Un pain au chocolat» and «une chocolatine» are both mentioned. What does the lesson say they are? | Two kinds of storm | *The same pastry | A cake for Epiphany | Two different breads
test: What is a pastry chef? | *A cook who makes pastries and pies | A person who sells sweets in the street | A cake with cream on top | A French baker only
test: «Un éclair is a flash of lightning and also a chocolate pastry.» What is true? | Éclair means thunder | Éclair means storm | Éclair is a French dance | *Éclair has two meanings: lightning and a pastry
exam: Which sentence uses pastry as the dough, not as a food you can count? | Ella saw three pastries in the window. | Dad ate a pastry with his tea. | *Mum rolled out the pastry for the apple pie. | Mum bought two pastries from the shop.
